    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We note that $x^{2^n} = (x^4)^{n/2} = y_n^2,$ so $x^{2^n} - x^{-2^n} = (x^{-2^n} - x^{2^n})(y_n^2 - y_n^{-2}) = 2(y_n^2 - y_n^{-2}).$ Dividing top and bottom by $x^{2^{n+1}},$ we find $y_n^{-2} = y_n^2(y_n^2 - 1) = y_n^4 - y_n^2,$ so $y_n^{-2} = y_n^2 \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{y_n^2}\\right),$ so $y_n^{-2} = y_n^2 - 1$ if $y_n^2 = 1.$  Then our sum becomes\n\\[\\sum_{n = 0}^\\infty \\frac{1}{2^2(y_n^2 - 1)}.\\]This sum is telescoping, so its terms all cancel, except the first one.  Therefore, the sum evaluates to $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}.$. The answer is \\frac{1}{4}",
